<clickhouse>
    <!--
      ClickHouse's own self-instrumentation, bounded.

      THE MEASUREMENT (2026-09-12): this workspace's ClickHouse held 19.83 GiB
      of system.* tables and 98 MiB of the telemetry SigNoz exists to store —
      the database's notes about itself were 200x the data. system.trace_log
      alone was 13.65 GiB across 781,359,938 rows, and it had grown large
      enough that `SELECT count() FROM system.trace_log` died with
      MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED: the table could no longer be queried at all.

      It grew without bound because none of these tables ship a TTL. The app's
      own retention setting (AW_SIGNOZ_RETENTION_DAYS, applied by
      entrypoint.sh) governs signoz_traces/signoz_logs/signoz_metrics — the
      data this app ingests. It never reached the ones ClickHouse writes about
      itself, and nothing else did either.

      WHY CONFIG RATHER THAN THE ENTRYPOINT'S ALTER: the SigNoz tables are
      created by otelcol's migrations, so config cannot declare them and an
      ALTER after the fact is the only hook. These tables are the opposite —
      ClickHouse creates them FROM this config, so declaring their lifetime
      here is the native mechanism and applies from the first row written,
      with no window in which an unbounded table exists.

      Disabled vs kept is a judgement about what is worth its bytes on a
      single-node, one-workspace deployment, not a blanket cull:
    -->

    <!--
      trace_log: the query profiler — stack samples, several per query. It is
      for diagnosing ClickHouse's own performance, which is not a thing anyone
      does here, and it was 69% of the total on its own.
    -->
    <trace_log remove="1"/>

    <!--
      text_log: ClickHouse's log lines, written into a table. The same lines
      already go to the container log, where they are actually read. This is a
      duplicate that costs gigabytes.
    -->
    <text_log remove="1"/>

    <!--
      asynchronous_metric_log: one row per background metric per second —
      257,334,058 rows for 231 MiB. The same values are visible live in
      system.asynchronous_metrics; the history is what nobody consults.
    -->
    <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>

    <!--
      The three below are KEPT, because they are what you actually want when
      ClickHouse misbehaves: what ran (query_log), what the server was doing
      (metric_log), and what the merges did (part_log). Three days is long
      enough to investigate an incident and short enough to stay bounded.

      ttl_only_drop_parts matches what entrypoint.sh already sets on the
      SigNoz tables: expired parts are DROPPED whole rather than rewritten
      row-by-row, so expiry costs almost nothing.
    -->
    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <ttl_only_drop_parts>1</ttl_only_drop_parts>
    </query_log>
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <ttl_only_drop_parts>1</ttl_only_drop_parts>
    </metric_log>
    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <ttl_only_drop_parts>1</ttl_only_drop_parts>
    </part_log>
</clickhouse>
